          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"The rose\",\"addressee\":\"The little prince\",\"anchor_quote\":\"je t'aime\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The rose directly avows her present loving emotional state to the little prince. The subsequent statement that he had not known it presents the love as a previously concealed fact, making truth-presenting avowal the expression's dominant force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tu n'en a rien su, par ma faute.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The focal expression avows love but does not itself undertake future conduct, impose an obligation, or renew a relational commitment. The rose's surrounding wishes and instructions must not be transferred onto the marked words.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tâche d'être heureux.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"Although spoken during an emotionally painful farewell, the expression is represented as a calm, intelligible disclosure rather than a reflex-like or affectively discharged cry. The concealed tears establish emotion but not expressive-discharge force in the focal words.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Il ne comprenait pas cette douceur calme.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Car elle ne voulait pas qu'il la vît pleurer.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The expression's core function is adequately captured as a direct avowal of love under T. Its contextual functions of confession, correction, and revelation do not constitute a separate core mode beyond that avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tu n'en a rien su, par ma faute.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"During the little prince's departure, after apologizing and wishing him happiness, the rose calmly reveals that she loves him and acknowledges that her own conduct prevented him from knowing it. He is present and listening, but the supplied scene gives no explicit verbal response to the avowal itself.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal expression contains no undertaking: the rose avows an emotional state rather than promising conduct or continued relationship. Her neighbouring words release him to pursue happiness and departure, but they are separate from the target. No explicit acceptance, rejection, or other uptake of the love avowal is supplied.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"I recognize the supplied passages as chapters VIII–IX of Antoine de Saint-Exupéry's Le Petit Prince (The Little Prince). The classification relies only on the supplied evidence.\",\"limitations\":\"The passages strongly represent the rose's affection through the narrator's references to her tenderness and concealed crying, but they do not independently establish every aspect of sincerity, duration, or mutual understanding. No omitted context is assumed to be absent from the work.\"}"
        }
